The decision, verified by structure:

```css
/* GRID — the layout is a COORDINATE SYSTEM you declare */
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr;         /* sidebar + main */
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;         /* header / body / footer */
  grid-template-areas: "header header"
                       "sidebar main"
                       "footer footer";       /* names > line numbers */
  min-height: 100vh;
}
.header  { grid-area: header; }   /* placement by NAME */
.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }

/* FLEXBOX — the layout is a DISTRIBUTION along one axis */
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;   /* logo left, actions right */
  align-items: center;
  gap: 12px;                          /* spacing, no margins */
}
```

The three-question test (the interview answer):
1. Do rows and columns need to ALIGN with each other? → Grid. (Flexbox wraps independently — line 2 doesn't know line 1's column positions.)
2. Does the CONTAINER define the structure, or does the CONTENT? Container-defined → Grid; content-driven → Flex.
3. Do you need to place items at specific coordinates / overlap them? → Grid only (grid-area placement, z-index layering, item spanning 2 columns while its neighbor spans 3 rows).

The composition pattern (what real codebases look like):
```
Grid: page shell (sidebar / main / header / footer — 2D)
  └─ Flex: navbar (logo left, menu right — 1D)
  └─ Grid: card grid inside main (3-column, aligned — 2D)
       └─ Flex: each card's title/meta/actions (1D)
```

The gap property note: both support gap now — the margin-collision/spacing-cleanup win that used to need lobotomized owl selectors.
